    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Decorators provide a clear, readable way to add metadata to methods.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0</defaultgrade>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <correctfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is correct.</p>]]></text></correctfeedback>
    <partiallycorrectfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is partially correct.</p>]]></text></partiallycorrectfeedback>
    <incorrectfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is incorrect.</p>]]></text></incorrectfeedback>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<code>@abc</code>]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. You import from <code>abc</code>, but the decorator has a specific name.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<code>@abstractmethod</code>]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Correct! This tells Python that this method is mandatory for all concrete subclasses.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<code>@virtual</code>]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. <code>virtual</code> is a keyword in C++, but not a standard decorator in Python.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<code>@must_implement</code>]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. This is not a standard Python decorator.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>

  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q15: Is-a vs Has-a relationship</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>If you have a class <code>Smartphone</code> and a class <code>Camera</code>, which relationship is more appropriate for a modern design?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Modeling a smartphone as a "type of camera" is a common design error.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0</defaultgrade>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <correctfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is correct.</p>]]></text></correctfeedback>
    <partiallycorrectfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is partially correct.</p>]]></text></partiallycorrectfeedback>
    <incorrectfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer is incorrect.</p>]]></text></incorrectfeedback>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Inheritance (Smartphone IS-A Camera).]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. A smartphone is much more than a camera, and it doesn't share the same behavioral identity.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Composition (Smartphone HAS-A Camera).]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Correct! A smartphone is composed of several modules, including a camera.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Both are equally appropriate.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. Composition is clearly superior for modeling component-based systems.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Neither, they should be global variables.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Incorrect. This would be a step back into procedural spaghetti code.</p>]]></text></feedback>
    </answer>
